{"name":"Copper Pipe","hex":"#da8f67","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"copper","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A reddish-brown metallic chemical element (symbol Cu) with the atomic number 29; also, the metal made up of this element.","The reddish-brown colour of copper (etymology 1, noun sense 1)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of copper (etymology 1, noun sense 1).","Having the reddish-brown colour of copper."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/copper"},{"term":"pipe","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Meanings relating to a wind instrument.\n A wind instrument consisting of a tube, often lined with holes to allow for adjustment in pitch, sounded by blowing into the tube. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}\n\n  A tube used to produce sound in an organ; an organ pipe. \n\nThe key or sound of the voice. \n\nA high-pitched sound, especially of a bird.","A wind instrument consisting of a tube, often lined with holes to allow for adjustment in pitch, sounded by blowing into the tube. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To play (music) on a pipe instrument, such as a bagpipe or a flute.","To shout loudly and at high pitch."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pipe"}],"entries":[{"title":"Copper tubing","blocks":[{"text":"Copper tubing is available in two basic types of tube—plumbing tube and air conditioning/refrigeration (ACR) tube, and in both drawn (hard) and annealed (soft) tempers. Because of its high level of corrosion resistance, it is used for water distribution systems, oil fuel transfer lines, non-flammable medical-gas systems, and as a refrigerant line in HVAC systems. Copper tubing is joined using flare connection, compression connection, pressed connection, or solder."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Rorskararemedror.jpg/330px-Rorskararemedror.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper_tubing","wikidata":"Q5168805"},{"title":"Pipe (fluid conveyance)","blocks":[{"text":"A pipe is a tubular section or hollow cylinder, usually but not necessarily of circular cross-section, used mainly to convey substances which can flow — liquids and gases (fluids), slurries, powders and masses of small solids. It can also be used for structural applications; a hollow pipe is far stiffer per unit weight than the solid members."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Series_of_tubes_on_top_of_Shibuya_Stream_-89062.jpeg/330px-Series_of_tubes_on_top_of_Shibuya_Stream_-89062.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pipe_(fluid_conveyance)","wikidata":"Q41551"},{"title":"Copper","blocks":[{"text":"Copper is a chemical element; it has symbol Cu and atomic number 29. It is a soft, malleable, and ductile metal with very high thermal and electrical conductivity. A freshly exposed surface of pure copper has a pinkish-orange color. Copper is used as a conductor of heat and electricity, as a building material, and as a constituent of various metal alloys, such as sterling silver used in jewelry, cupronickel used to make marine hardware and coins, and constantan used in strain gauges and thermocouples for temperature measurement."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/NatCopper.jpg/330px-NatCopper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper","wikidata":"Q753"},{"title":"Copper (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Copper is a reddish brown color that resembles the metal copper. The first recorded use of copper as a color name in English was in 1594."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Copper-18706.jpg/330px-Copper-18706.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper_(color)","wikidata":"Q2722041"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Propane_torch_soldering_copper_pipe.jpg/500px-Propane_torch_soldering_copper_pipe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Propane_torch_soldering_copper_pipe.jpg/1280px-Propane_torch_soldering_copper_pipe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Propane_torch_soldering_copper_pipe.jpg/500px-Propane_torch_soldering_copper_pipe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Propane torch soldering copper tube","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APropane_torch_soldering_copper_pipe.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Paris_-_Palais_du_Louvre_-_PA00085992_-_1090.jpg/500px-Paris_-_Palais_du_Louvre_-_PA00085992_-_1090.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Paris_-_Palais_du_Louvre_-_PA00085992_-_1090.jpg/1280px-Paris_-_Palais_du_Louvre_-_PA00085992_-_1090.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Paris_-_Palais_du_Louvre_-_PA00085992_-_1090.jpg/500px-Paris_-_Palais_du_Louvre_-_PA00085992_-_1090.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Drain pipe of the Louvre","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AParis_-_Palais_du_Louvre_-_PA00085992_-_1090.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Encanamento.JPG/500px-Encanamento.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Encanamento.JP